diagnosis_text:
A complete four-phase diagnostic sweep of the simple_bgp lab found no fault on any device or path. Phase 1 reachability showed 0% packet loss and sub-1 ms round-trip times in both directions (pc1→pc2 and pc2→pc1), directly contradicting the user's claim of "huge latency." The inter-AS peering link (router1:eth0 ↔ router2:eth0) carries normal traffic without any shaping. The tc_snapshot confirmed that all six interfaces across all four devices run only the default fq_codel qdisc — no tbf bandwidth limiter and no netem delay, jitter, or corruption module is present on any interface, including the BGP peering link. The bgp_snapshot confirmed both FRR processes are healthy (watchfrr, zebra, bgpd all active), the eBGP session between AS 1 and AS 2 is fully Established, and each router is advertising its correct prefix (router1: 195.11.14.0/24, router2: 200.1.1.0/24). The infra_sweep found no ACL drop rules, no addressing anomalies, and no resolver misconfiguration. The l2_snapshot found no duplicate MACs and no down interfaces. The pressure_sweep found no stress or flood tool running on any device. The user's perception of slowness has no corresponding network-layer mechanism in the current lab state; there is nothing to restart. Root cause: no fault exists — the network is operating normally and the user report is unconfirmed by measurement.
=== END ===